WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.590
Since the Hubble Space Telescope went into
orbit back in April 1990,

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:06.440
it has sent back a ton of incredible photos.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:10.470
Each has its own story,
but one of Hubble’s greatest images is this

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.550
one, from 1995.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:17.190
This is a snapshot of nearly the entire history
of the universe - and the first of its kind.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:28.190
The Hubble Deep Field is an unusual image
that came from an unusual process.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:31.880
Typically astronomers apply to use Hubble
to look at a particular known object.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.870
“You want to study a star?

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:37.511
Okay you know what star you’re going to
study. You point the telescope at that star.”

00:00:37.511 --> 00:00:38.511
That’s Robert Williams.

00:00:38.511 --> 00:00:41.431
He became the director of the telescope in
1993.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.899
It was his decision to create the Deep Field
image by pointing the telescope at nothing

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:46.899
in particular.

00:00:46.899 --> 00:00:53.559
“What we're doing basically was just the
opposite — we're trying to find a sort of

00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:58.930
indiscriminate area of sky where no observation
had been made before.”

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:02.780
They wanted to test how well Hubble could
survey very distant galaxies.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.379
But they didn’t know what they’d see.

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:07.109
And It wasn’t a great time to be trying
new things.

00:01:07.109 --> 00:01:17.560
“After spending 2 billion dollars for 12
years, to have this kind of unexpected, very

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.689
large mistake take place..”

00:01:20.689 --> 00:01:24.810
The Hubble team was still repairing the reputation
of the telescope after a flaw in the main

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.289
mirror produced blurry images for nearly three years.

00:01:28.289 --> 00:01:33.420
“We were the brunt of jokes and the newspapers,
political cartoons you know Johnny Carson

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.359
show. NASA was being made fun of
for having made such a monumental screw up

00:01:38.359 --> 00:01:41.959
of such an expensive project.”

00:01:41.959 --> 00:01:46.270
NASA sent astronauts on a 5-day mission to
install a module that would fix the problem.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:51.200
And it worked.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.329
So Williams’ team spent 1995 planning the
deep field observation.

00:01:55.329 --> 00:01:58.380
For one thing, they had to decide where to
point the telescope.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:02.770
The goal was to see far beyond our galaxy,
so the spot needed to be away from the galactic

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:06.569
plane of the Milky Way and away from any known
large galaxy clusters.

00:02:06.569 --> 00:02:08.939
They didn’t want anything bright to block
the view.

00:02:08.939 --> 00:02:13.120
And to get continuous observations,
it needed to be a location that wouldn’t

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.739
be obscured when Hubble went around Earth,
as it does every hour and a half or so.

00:02:17.739 --> 00:02:22.680
They settled on a region just above the big
dipper -- a dark, unremarkable, peephole into

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.030
the universe.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.599
The field of view was extremely narrow.

00:02:26.599 --> 00:02:30.680
Astronomers measure the apparent size of objects
in the sky in angular degrees, and a degree

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.540
can be divided into 60 arcminutes.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:37.790
From Earth, the moon is about half a degree
across, around 30 arcminutes.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:41.560
But the area that Hubble photographed was
just 2.6 arcminutes across.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.030
“A little larger than a pinhead at arm's
length.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.329
So it’s a teeny patch of sky.”

00:02:46.329 --> 00:02:52.420
The observations began on December, 18, 1995,
collecting 4 different wavelengths of light.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:59.370
And over the next 10 days the telescope took
342 images of that teeny patch of sky.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:06.129
“We were relieved that we are getting good
data but we had to keep adding it up and so

00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:10.010
it wasn't until ten days that we realized oh, we really got something.”

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:14.330
There are a few nearby stars in the image
but pretty much all the other objects here,

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:18.049
including these tiny blue dots -- they’re galaxies.

00:03:18.049 --> 00:03:21.549
The light from these different galaxies has
been traveling for vastly different amounts

00:03:21.549 --> 00:03:26.530
of time so the furthest galaxies are shown
pretty early in their evolution, more than

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:27.620
12 billion years ago.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:30.319
That’s just a billion and a half years after
the big bang.

00:03:30.319 --> 00:03:34.810
It’s as if you could point a telescope across
the earth and actually photograph ancient

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:39.780
Egypt, with a neanderthal in the background
and then further back there’s a dinosaur.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.740
The research team sampled another tiny spot
two years later, this time in the southern sky.

00:03:44.749 --> 00:03:48.859
“We wanted to know okay then we got one
spot of this you never know maybe it was some

00:03:48.859 --> 00:03:54.859
weird spot and so we thought it was important
to repeat the observation.”

00:03:54.859 --> 00:03:57.879
The datasets boosted estimates of the total
number of galaxies.

00:03:57.879 --> 00:04:02.180
They allowed researchers to track the history
of star formation through the universe.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:06.249
And they helped confirm the bottom-up theory
of galaxy formation, by revealing galaxies

00:04:06.249 --> 00:04:09.779
that are small and irregular early in their
evolution.

00:04:09.779 --> 00:04:13.470
But one of the most important legacies of
the Hubble Deep Field is how it changed the

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:14.889
culture of astronomy.

00:04:14.889 --> 00:04:22.450
“Until this time astronomy had a history
of people taking the data and keeping it to

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:27.000
themselves until they had fully analyzed it
after all this was intellectual property.”

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.840
Instead of hoarding the discoveries embedded
in the dataset, Williams and his team formatted

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.800
and released it immediately to the wider scientific
community.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.430
It’s been cited in hundreds of papers.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:44.000
“Nowadays it is so much more common for
people to take interesting observations and

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:49.050
make the data available to the public even
though they might have a right to keep it

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.400
for a certain period of time to themselves.”

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:55.610
Thanks to servicing missions that installed
more advanced cameras, Hubble has since made

00:04:55.610 --> 00:05:00.190
even deeper deep field images, and those data
too, were released to the public.

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:06.690
“I think it it moves forward the march of
human understanding human knowledge tremendously

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:08.470
and the Hubble deep field did that.”

